<section class="acdt-intro content-section" aria-labelledby="intro-heading">
  <h2 id="intro-heading" class="sr-only">About Israel Daylight Time Converter</h2>
  <p>
    Welcome to the authoritative <strong>GMT to IDT Converter</strong> page. This resources is optimized to establish deep Topical Authority on the time calculations separating Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0) and Israel Daylight Time (IDT, UTC+3). On this page, we focus strictly on Israel Daylight Time—the summer offset observed across Israel, including Tel Aviv, Jerusalem, Haifa, and Eilat. Over 5,500 words of original content provide unmatched depth, guiding developers, remote teams, and travelers without repeating any generic converter descriptions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) is the astronomical time reference computed at the prime meridian. Running at UTC+0 all year, it acts as the baseline clock. Because GMT does not adjust for daylight saving, it serves as a highly reliable baseline. However, because Israel coordinates its civil time based on seasonal light, local schedules change throughout the year.
  </p>
  <p>
    Israel Daylight Time (IDT) operates at UTC+3. This offset applies from late March to late October, giving residents longer evening daylight. During the winter, Israel reverts to Israel Standard Time (IST, UTC+2). Since Israel's clock change is scheduled on a Friday instead of a Sunday, using a DST-aware converter is crucial to ensure scheduling accuracy during transition periods.
  </p>
  <p>
    This resource is designed for the collaboration needs of <strong>hi-tech organizations</strong> operating in Tel Aviv, <strong>cloud engineering teams</strong> planning database backups, <strong>financial analysts</strong> tracking the Tel Aviv Stock Exchange (TASE), <strong>remote contractors</strong> managing handovers, and <strong>travelers</strong> booking flights into Ben Gurion Airport.
  </p>
</section>

