<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES & CONFUSIONS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Confusions and Pitfalls in GMT to IDT Calculations</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>Confusing IDT with IST (Indian Standard Time)</h3>
    <p>
      The abbreviation "IST" is shared by both Israel Standard Time (UTC+2) and Indian Standard Time (UTC+5:30). When search engines or automated systems resolve "IST," they sometimes output Indian Standard Time instead of Israel winter time. This can cause a significant 3.5-hour scheduling error. During summer, the daylight saving label "IDT" helps prevent this confusion.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>The Friday Transition Day Difference</h3>
    <p>
      While most countries transition to daylight saving time on Sunday mornings, Israel makes the change on Friday morning at 2:00 AM. This prevents conflicts with Sabbath observers on Saturday evening. Automated systems handle this transition, but manual calendar scheduling during the last week of March requires careful attention.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>Assuming Israel follows EU Daylight Saving Dates</h3>
    <p>
      Although Israel's clock change dates are close to the European Union's schedule, they do not always match perfectly. The EU transitions on the last Sunday of March, while Israel transitions on the Friday before the last Sunday. This can create a brief window where the relative offsets between London/Brussels and Tel Aviv shift.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

